"""Replace repeated prose with a continuous argument within the content budget."""
from pathlib import Path
def change(s,a,b):
    assert s.count(a)==1,(a[:80],s.count(a));return s.replace(a,b)
p=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]/'manuscript/main.tex';s=p.read_text()
s=change(s,'Reward models and translation metrics select language outputs, but evaluator changes can alter the selected set','Reward models and translation metrics select language outputs whose membership can change with evaluators')
s=change(s,'Consider a batch selected by a fixed evaluator pool and cutoff. A curator may retain some selections and defer others for review, trading retained count against replacement sensitivity.','For a batch selected by a fixed pool and cutoff, a curator can retain selections or defer decisions for review, trading count against replacement sensitivity.')
s=change(s,'We ask how much score evidence certifies near-optimal retention under a budget on average membership changes. Maps and cutoff stay fixed across a finite family of single-evaluator replacements. A commitment mask identifies accepted baseline decisions across that family. Retention has no fixed-size or per-prompt quota; the budget averages over replacements.','We ask how much score evidence certifies near-optimal retention under a budget on average membership changes. A commitment mask identifies accepted baseline decisions across a finite family of single-evaluator replacements, with fixed maps and cutoff. Retention has no fixed-size or per-prompt quota.')
s=change(s,'NLP replays compare discovery with matching minima. Joint evidence improves on separate proofs in small mean-pooling batches; full-batch interventions show that true row totals need not reduce queries. Controlled panels vary column sharing; protocol controls change the task.','Replays compare discovery with matching minima. Joint evidence improves on separate proofs in small mean-pooling batches; full-batch interventions show that true row totals need not reduce queries. Sharing and protocol controls clarify these comparisons.')
s=change(s,'Costly input queries appear in priced information and Boolean evaluation \\citep{charikar2002priced,deshpande2016sbfe,gkenosis2018ssclass}, and robust selection \\citep{goerigk2015robustqueries,chen2026selection}. Weight-query knapsack reveals an item\\textquotesingle s weight; our entry reveals one contribution to a row\\textquotesingle s multi-replacement cost. Closest to our evidence decomposition, knapsack verification separates solution and exclusion evidence for uncertain profits \\citep{schloter2025knapsack}. Our median model has unit output value and opposition-count cost. Sorting gives the full-information optimum; entries or shared columns certify it. Shared revelation also occurs in covering models \\citep{megow2023set}.','Costly queries occur in priced information, Boolean evaluation \\citep{charikar2002priced,deshpande2016sbfe,gkenosis2018ssclass} and robust selection \\citep{goerigk2015robustqueries,chen2026selection}. Knapsack verification separates solution and exclusion evidence for uncertain profits \\citep{schloter2025knapsack}. Our outputs instead have unit value; entries reveal contributions to their opposition-count costs. Sorting gives the full-information optimum, certified by entries or shared columns. Covering models also use shared revelation \\citep{megow2023set}.')
s=change(s,'Weight-region ranking stability studies scoring-weight perturbations \\citep{asudeh2018stable}; we replace evaluators and certify retained count at a fixed cutoff. Necessary/possible top-$k$ winners concern preference completions \\citep{imber2020topk}; our objective is retention under a fixed cutoff. PAC selection estimates reward means \\citep{kalyanakrishnan2012pac}, whereas our queries reveal fixed scores. Middleware top-$k$ uses sorted and sometimes random access \\citep{fagin2003optimal}; our oracle has no sorted interface. Pruning, ensembles and ranking exits reduce scoring in other settings \\citep{li2022pruning,inoue2019ensemble,busolin2021exit}. Best-of-Evidence reuses checks to choose candidates \\citep{zhang2026bestofevidencebestofnselectionpartial}; we certify churn feasibility and count regret.','Ranking stability studies scoring-weight regions \\citep{asudeh2018stable}, and necessary/possible top-$k$ winners concern preference completions \\citep{imber2020topk}; we replace evaluators at a fixed cutoff. Unlike PAC reward estimation \\citep{kalyanakrishnan2012pac}, queries reveal fixed scores. Unlike middleware top-$k$ \\citep{fagin2003optimal}, our oracle has no sorted interface. Pruning, ensembles and ranking exits reduce scoring \\citep{li2022pruning,inoue2019ensemble,busolin2021exit}; Best-of-Evidence reuses checks for candidate selection \\citep{zhang2026bestofevidencebestofnselectionpartial}. Our target is churn feasibility and count regret.')
s=change(s,'For nonnegative costs $w$, define the selected-retention capacity','For nonnegative costs $w$, define retention capacity')
s=change(s,'Our completion model imposes no cross-entry constraints on unknown scores. Zero-flip endpoints jointly realize $\\ell$; maximum-flip endpoints jointly realize $u$. Thus $V^+$ and $V^-$ are attainable upper and lower retention bounds for both pooling rules, without a statistical independence assumption. Median Boolean costs admit closed-form minima; for mean pooling, a joint evidence program below preserves queries that improve both bounds.','Without cross-entry constraints, zero-flip endpoints jointly realize $\\ell$ and maximum-flip endpoints realize $u$. Both retention bounds are attainable, without statistical independence. Median Boolean costs admit closed-form minima; the mean joint program counts queries improving both bounds.')
s=change(s,'The level $t^*$ is the smallest opposition cap that makes the cheapest $p$ rows exceed budget; it is distinct from the selection cutoff $t_0$.','The opposition cap $t^*$ makes the cheapest $p$ rows exceed budget; it differs from selection cutoff $t_0$.')
s=change(s,'The certificate holds for any acquisition order. We compare four next-query rules with shared interval updates, mask solver and stopping criterion.','Four acquisition rules share interval updates, mask solver and stopping criterion.')
s=change(s,'At each update, sorting active selected costs computes a retained-count capacity in $O(m\\log m)$ time; invariant selected rows add zero churn cost. A histogram computes the same capacities for small integer-cost ranges. Each entry query is charged once; all $M$ replacement outcomes are computed from that score.','Sorting active costs takes $O(m\\log m)$ per update; invariant selected rows add zero cost. Histograms suffice for small integer-cost ranges. Each queried score is charged once and determines all $M$ replacement outcomes.')
s=change(s,'Full-information controls vary the selection task or guarantee. All-decision cardinality can spend budget on exclusions; selected retention spends it on returned outputs. Uniform thresholds include entire cost ties.','Full-information controls change the task or guarantee: all-decision cardinality spends budget on exclusions, selected retention on returned outputs, and uniform thresholds include cost ties.')
s=change(s,'With mean pooling, reference priority inspects 85.4--86.5\\% across these panels.','Mean reference priority inspects 85.4--86.5\\% across these panels.')
s=change(s,'Across-depth prediction correlations confound changes in cutoff, rows and target (Appendix~\\ref{app:acquisition-diagnostics}); the interventions below keep each task fixed.','Depth confounds cutoff, rows and target in prediction correlations (Appendix~\\ref{app:acquisition-diagnostics}); interventions below keep the task fixed.')
s=change(s,'Low median inspection largely coincides with high initial coverage; it alone does not establish a superior query order.','Low median inspection coincides with high initial coverage, without establishing a superior query order.')
p.write_text(s)
